 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses 2Q replacement with two queues: a probationary queue for first-use sheets and a protected queue for sheets accessed again while cached. A newly admitted sheet enters the probationary queue; a subsequent access to the same key promotes it to the protected queue. Eviction draws from the probationary queue before the protected queue, so a long sequential screening recycles through probationary entries without displacing the comparison sheets editors revisit. Both queues and their bookkeeping are private to the worker; the fixed HTTP interface, render-queue concurrency, and 12 GiB disk bound are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache replacement policy | 2Q: a probationary queue for first-use sheets and a protected queue for reused sheets, promoting on a second access | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q isolates one-off screening scans in the probationary queue so they cannot evict the small set of comparison sheets editors revisit. The extra queue and split bookkeeping stays inside the worker's private cache and leaves the fixed interface, render-queue concurrency, and 12 GiB disk bound unchanged. LRU lets a long sequential screening displace that reused working set. Aged frequency keeps yesterday's popular clips too long after editors switch projects and adds counter maintenance and aging. |
